{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3191","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3191","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3191\",\"parent_revision\":\"612669938fd8d66da9e4d682e259d3efe55ff1bdaf2459f312f8379144e95b2d\",\"root_revision\":\"612669938fd8d66da9e4d682e259d3efe55ff1bdaf2459f312f8379144e95b2d\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-23\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n`skills/complete-implementation/references/concerns-processing.md` declares itself authoritative and non-negotiable at line 8: \\\"the authoritative procedure for Pre-Phase 1b Process Accumulated Concerns. Execute steps in the exact order shown, including branches, decision points, and stop conditions.\\\"\\n\\nTwo defects sit in the nine-node flowchart it prescribes. Both verified from source.\\n\\n## Defect 1 \u2014 the read cannot answer the question the next node asks\\n\\nLine 12 prescribes:\\n\\n```\\nbacklog_view(selector='#{issue}')\\n```\\n\\n`summary` defaults to `True` (`backlog_core/server.py:2516`: \\\"Use summary=False to receive the full response; summary=True (default) returns...\\\"), so this returns the compact routing manifest built by the function at `server.py:2243`, which carries metadata and a sections index \u2014 not entry content.\\n\\nThe immediately following decision node asks:\\n\\n> Does item have a '## Concerns' section with unchecked items ('- [ ]')?\\n\\nThe checkbox text is not in the response the previous node requested. The workflow cannot be executed as written from the data it fetches. An agent following it either improvises a second call \u2014 which the file forbids \u2014 or answers the decision from a response that cannot contain the answer.\\n\\n## Defect 2 \u2014 the write appends instead of replacing, and the stale copy drives the next run\\n\\nLine 22 prescribes:\\n\\n```\\nbacklog_groom(selector='#{issue}', section='Concerns', content='{updated checklist}')\\n```\\n\\nNo `entry_id`, no `replace_section`. That reaches the default branch at `operations.py:915-918`:\\n\\n```python\\n# Default: append only when content is non-empty and not already present.\\nif groomed_content.strip() and not any(e.content == groomed_content and not e.struck for e in section.entries):\\n    section.entries.append(Entry(id=now_iso(), content=groomed_content))\\n```\\n\\nThe updated checklist differs from the original by definition \u2014 that is the point of updating it \u2014 so the idempotence guard does not fire and the content is appended. The Concerns section then holds the original all-unchecked checklist and the new all-checked checklist, both unstruck, as peers.\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\nThe two defects compound across runs. The next invocation reads the section, finds the stale all-unchecked entry still present and still unstruck, concludes unchecked concerns exist, and reprocesses every concern already handled \u2014 filing a fresh `backlog_add` for each one, per the `CheckOff` node. Each run appends another checklist copy, so the stale evidence accumulates rather than clearing.\\n\\nThat is self-amplifying: duplicate backlog items on every completion pass, growing with each run, with no signal that the concerns were already processed.\\n\\n## Not established\\n\\nWhether this has produced duplicate items in the tracker already. Scanning for repeated items whose source reads \\\"Quality vigilance concern from #N\\\" would establish it. Whether Defect 1 is currently masked by agents improvising the `summary=False` call rather than following the file literally \u2014 which would mean the workflow works in practice while being unexecutable as specified, and would still need fixing, since the file forbids exactly that improvisation.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\nThe append-instead-of-replace behaviour and the disclosure surface dropping `struck` (#3187) are the same underlying problem seen from two sides: the write path creates entry pairs that the read path cannot distinguish.\\n\\n## User-provided context\\n\\nFor Defect 2, `replace_section=True` with a reason, or `entry_id` targeting the existing checklist, are both visible directions \u2014 but they behave differently under #3187, since `replace_section` produces exactly the struck/active pair that the disclosure path flattens. For Defect 1, either the workflow needs `summary=False` or the compact manifest needs to answer presence-and-state; the latter is a design decision about the tool surface, not a workflow fix.\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThe two defects compound across runs. The next invocation reads the section, finds the stale all-unchecked entry still present and still unstruck, concludes unchecked concerns exist, and reprocesses every concern already handled \u2014 filing a fresh `backlog_add` for each one, per the `CheckOff` node. Each run appends another checklist copy, so the stale evidence accumulates rather than clearing.\\n\\nThat is self-amplifying: duplicate backlog items on every completion pass, growing with each run, with no signal that the concerns were already processed.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation during caller-requirement analysis, 2026-08-23\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-23\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## User Provided Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nFor Defect 2, `replace_section=True` with a reason, or `entry_id` targeting the existing checklist, are both visible directions \u2014 but they behave differently under #3187, since `replace_section` produces exactly the struck/active pair that the disclosure path flattens. For Defect 1, either the workflow needs `summary=False` or the compact manifest needs to answer presence-and-state; the latter is a design decision about the tool surface, not a workflow fix.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Defect 2 The Write Appends Instead Of Replacing And The Stale Copy Drives The Next Run\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nLine 22 prescribes:\\n\\n```\\nbacklog_groom(selector='#{issue}', section='Concerns', content='{updated checklist}')\\n```\\n\\nNo `entry_id`, no `replace_section`. That reaches the default branch at `operations.py:915-918`:\\n\\n```python\\n# Default: append only when content is non-empty and not already present.\\nif groomed_content.strip() and not any(e.content == groomed_content and not e.struck for e in section.entries):\\n    section.entries.append(Entry(id=now_iso(), content=groomed_content))\\n```\\n\\nThe updated checklist differs from the original by definition \u2014 that is the point of updating it \u2014 so the idempotence guard does not fire and the content is appended. The Concerns section then holds the original all-unchecked checklist and the new all-checked checklist, both unstruck, as peers.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Defect 1 The Read Cannot Answer The Question The Next Node Asks\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nLine 12 prescribes:\\n\\n```\\nbacklog_view(selector='#{issue}')\\n```\\n\\n`summary` defaults to `True` (`backlog_core/server.py:2516`: \\\"Use summary=False to receive the full response; summary=True (default) returns...\\\"), so this returns the compact routing manifest built by the function at `server.py:2243`, which carries metadata and a sections index \u2014 not entry content.\\n\\nThe immediately following decision node asks:\\n\\n> Does item have a '## Concerns' section with unchecked items ('- [ ]')?\\n\\nThe checkbox text is not in the response the previous node requested. The workflow cannot be executed as written from the data it fetches. An agent following it either improvises a second call \u2014 which the file forbids \u2014 or answers the decision from a response that cannot contain the answer.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nT